Since $0 = f(-x) = f(x) - f(x)$, we have $f(x) = f(-x)$. When $x = 0$, we get $f(0) = f(0) - f(0)$, hence $f(0) = 0$. So, any solution is of the form $f(x) = A(x - c)(-x - c)$, where $A$ is some constant and $c$ is the point at which the function vanishes (its mirror image is at $-c$). However, the condition $f(-x) = -f(x)$ still holds: $A(-x + c)(x + c) = -A(x - c)(-x - c)$, which simplifies to $A((x + c)(-x + c) - (x - c)(x + c)) = 0$.  Since we want nontrivial solutions (when $A \neq 0$), we can assume the following relationship between $c$ and $A$:

\boxed{c = -\frac12}

Putting this result into the expression for $f(x)$, we get  $f(x) = A \left((x + (\frac12))(-x + (\frac12)) - (x - (\frac12))(x + (\frac12)) \right) = A(-x^2 + x + \frac14)$. The function defined as $f(x) = -x^2 + x + \frac14$ is the desired even-odd function that vanishes at $x = -\frac12$ and its mirror image.

\boxed{-\frac12, -\frac12}

Note that other solutions can be obtained by changing the sign of $A$, and the mirror image will be reflected about the $y$-axis. We will also obtain the negative values when we substitute $y = -x$. For example, $g(y) = -f(-y) = y^2 - y + \frac14$.

\boxed{-\frac12, \frac12}

