\begin{align*}
w^3 + 1 &= -z^3 \\\
w^3 &= -1 - z^3 \\
w &= \sqrt[3]{-1} \sqrt[3]{-1 - z^3}
\end{align*}

Now we have $z^2 = w$ and

\begin{align*}
z &= (\pm \omega_3)^2 \left( \sqrt[3]{-1} \sqrt[3]{-1 - (\pm \omega_3)^4 z^6} \right) \\
z^6 &= -1 - (\pm \omega_3)^4 z^6 \\\
z^6 & = \frac{-1}{1 - (\omega_3)^4} \\
z &= -\sqrt[6]{\frac{-1}{1 - (\omega_3)^4}} \left( \frac{(\pm \omega_3)}{\sqrt[3]{1 - (\omega_3)^4}} \right)^2
\end{align*}

where $\omega_3 = \frac{-1 + \sqrt{3}i}{2}$ is the cube root of unity. Now, we take the arguments of all possible roots:

\begin{align*}
\theta &= \arg (z) \\\
\theta &= \arg \left( -\left( 1 - (\omega_3)^4 \right)^{- \frac{1}{6}} \left( \frac{(\pm \omega_3)}{\sqrt[3]{1 - (\omega_3)^4}} \right)^2 \right) \\\
&= \arg \left( \frac{(\pm \omega_3)}{\sqrt[3]{1 - (\omega_3)^4}} \right) - 6 \cdot \arg( 1 - (\omega_3)^4 )
\end{align*}

The arguments of the cubes roots of unity are $\frac{2 \pi k}{3}$ for $k = 0, 1, 2$. Hence, within the first quadrant, we find the argument of the root we seek to be

